      "artist": "BlueHypercane761",
      "description": "Track map of the tornadoes of the 2021 Quad-State Tornado Family, outlined in blue. The other tornado tracks from the wider outbreak are outlined in red. The massive tornado outbreak was triggered by Winter Storm Atticus, and this tornado family was spawned by a single exceptionally long-lived supercell, which produced 2 long-tracked EF4 tornadoes, the 2021 Tri-State tornado and the 2021 Western Kentucky tornado. These were the most powerful and deadliest tornadoes of the Tornado outbreak of December 10–11, 2021. The Quad-State Tornado Family lasted for a total of 4 hours and 41 minutes, carved out a path 250 miles long, killed 66 people and injured 535 others, and caused around $3 billion (2021 USD) in damage, tracking across the states of Arkansas, Missouri, Tennessee, and Kentucky. Had the Quad-State Tornado been a single tornado, it would've broken the distance record set by the 1925 Tri-State Tornado, which lasted over 3 hours and 45 minutes, and carved out a path 235 miles long. This map was derived from Data:Tornadoes of December 10–11, 2021.map.",
